<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marib</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mariaba, Ma'rib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marib is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> on the edge of the Ramlat al-Sabatayn desert, capital of Marib <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>. In antiquity it was the capital of the kingdom of Saba, sustained by the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Marib Dam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade, and it has major ruins including the Awwam and Bar'an temples. Oil was found nearby in 1984, and in the recent civil war the city became a key government stronghold.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marib.jpg" alt="Marib" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: بدرالدين الشهاري · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MaribCity001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Marib lies in the region of the Sarawat Mountains, about 120 kilometres east of Sanaa. It is the capital of Marib Governorate. The ancient city, known as Mirwah, is one of the most important places for Arabian archaeology. In 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to the World Heritage List as one of the Landmarks of the Ancient Kingdom of Saba, Marib. The modern town stands about 3.5 kilometres north of the centre of the ancient city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Sabaean kingdom was based around Marib, with territory in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, and its kings made the city their capital. Some scholars believe Saba to be the Sheba of biblical fame. The Sabaean kings built great irrigation works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Marib Dam</a>, whose ruins are still visible and which supported a flourishing culture for more than a thousand years. They also built castles and temples in the area, most notably the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaqah">Almaqah</a> at Awwam and Barran. Saba was known for dealing in the frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a> trade.</p><p>In 25 BCE Aelius Gallus led a Roman expedition to Marib and laid siege to the city. He suffered heavy losses and was forced to retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The site of the ancient city was largely abandoned during the 20th century. A small village remains, but the multi-storey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> buildings of the historic city are mostly in ruins.</p><p>After floods ravaged the country in 1982, the president of the U.A.E., <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Sheikh Zayed</a> bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a>, financed the building of the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Marib Dam</a> in 1984. He was reportedly descended from people who migrated from the Marib area to what is now the U.A.E. A refinery opened in Marib in 1986, and an oil pipeline runs from the town to Ra's Isa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Marib has a hot desert climate. It had 16,794 inhabitants in 2005. Its ancient ruins, above all the dam and the temples at Awwam and Barran, are the chief survivals of the Sabaean past.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Walled towns, hilltop castles and fortified capitals such as Zafar and <b>Marib</b> anchored control of the land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heartland was the oasis of <b>Marib</b>, watered by the seasonal floods of the Wadi Dhana, and the nearby town of Sirwah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy depended on oil exported from the <b>Marib</b> field from 1987 and the Masila field in Hadramawt from 1993, which provided most government revenue, and on remittances from workers in the Gulf.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The largest commitment came in Yemen from 2015, where the UAE was the main partner of Saudi Arabia in the coalition against the Houthi movement; 45 Emirati soldiers died in a missile strike near <b>Marib</b> in September 2015.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ma'in arose in the al-Jawf valley north of <b>Marib</b>, in a region of city-states once subject to the Kingdom of Saba.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-main">Kingdom of Ma'in</a></li><li><blockquote>Remittances from workers in Saudi Arabia sustained the economy, and oil was found near <b>Marib</b> in 1984.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
